From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] Messenger: fix unaligned reads in Timestamp/FrameSize decode

Timestamp::Timestamp(const common::DataConstBuffer&) and
FrameSize::FrameSize(const common::DataConstBuffer&) decode wire
values by reinterpret_cast'ing a byte inside an arbitrarily-offset
const uint8_t* buffer to a reference of the target integer type
(uint64_t / uint16_t / uint32_t), then let the compiler emit a
native-width load straight from that address. Since the buffer is a
byte-offset view into a std::vector<uint8_t> backing store with no
alignment guarantee, this is undefined behavior (strict-aliasing
violation) and, on hardware without unaligned-access support for
multi-register loads, an actual functional bug.

On ARMv7 in particular, LDR/STR (single-register, word/halfword)
unaligned accesses are handled transparently by the hardware, but
LDRD/STRD/LDM/STM (double-word / 64-bit) accesses are not: an
unaligned uint64_t load faults into the kernel's alignment-trap
handler. Timestamp::Timestamp(const DataConstBuffer&) is invoked on
every audio/video media-with-timestamp frame
(VideoServiceChannel::handleAVMediaWithTimestampIndication,
AudioServiceChannel's equivalent), i.e. continuously while a media
channel is actively streaming, at a byte offset that is essentially
never 8-byte aligned. In practice this produces thousands of
kernel-handled alignment traps per minute of active playback on
affected hardware, and the resulting emulation overhead can starve
the transport/IO thread badly enough to trip the channel's own
timeout logic, tearing down the whole AA session mid-stream.

Fix both call sites (and the sibling FrameSize decode, which reads
uint16_t/uint32_t the same unsafe way -- those don't fault on ARMv7
since word/halfword unaligned access is handled in hardware, but it's
the same undefined-behavior bug) by memcpy'ing into a properly aligned
local variable instead of pointer-punning the raw buffer.

Upstream-Status: Pending

diff --git a/src/Messenger/FrameSize.cpp b/src/Messenger/FrameSize.cpp
index 352de2f..15d9efa 100644
--- a/src/Messenger/FrameSize.cpp
+++ b/src/Messenger/FrameSize.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 *  along with aasdk. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 */
 
+#include <cstring>
 #include <boost/endian/conversion.hpp>
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Messenger/FrameSize.hpp>
 
@@ -47,13 +48,17 @@ FrameSize::FrameSize(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer)
     if(buffer.size >= 2)
     {
         frameSizeType_ = FrameSizeType::SHORT;
-        frameSize_ = boost::endian::big_to_native(reinterpret_cast<const uint16_t&>(buffer.cdata[0]));
+        uint16_t frameSizeBig;
+        std::memcpy(&frameSizeBig, buffer.cdata, sizeof(frameSizeBig));
+        frameSize_ = boost::endian::big_to_native(frameSizeBig);
     }
 
     if(buffer.size >= 6)
     {
         frameSizeType_ = FrameSizeType::EXTENDED;
-        totalSize_ = boost::endian::big_to_native(reinterpret_cast<const uint32_t&>(buffer.cdata[2]));
+        uint32_t totalSizeBig;
+        std::memcpy(&totalSizeBig, buffer.cdata + 2, sizeof(totalSizeBig));
+        totalSize_ = boost::endian::big_to_native(totalSizeBig);
     }
 }
 
diff --git a/src/Messenger/Timestamp.cpp b/src/Messenger/Timestamp.cpp
index 5de4e44..7deaa3a 100644
--- a/src/Messenger/Timestamp.cpp
+++ b/src/Messenger/Timestamp.cpp
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 *  along with aasdk. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 */
 
+#include <cstring>
 #include <boost/endian/conversion.hpp>
 #include <f1x/aasdk/Messenger/Timestamp.hpp>
 
@@ -34,7 +35,8 @@ Timestamp::Timestamp(ValueType stamp)
 
 Timestamp::Timestamp(const common::DataConstBuffer& buffer)
 {
-    const ValueType& timestampBig = reinterpret_cast<const ValueType&>(buffer.cdata[0]);
+    ValueType timestampBig;
+    std::memcpy(&timestampBig, buffer.cdata, sizeof(timestampBig));
     stamp_ = boost::endian::big_to_native(timestampBig);
 }
 
